Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Center, EDSA comer Quezon Avenue. West Triangle, Quezon City http Ihaww_ dilg.gov.ph OILG OPINION NO. 18 JUN 2019 MS. ANA THERESA A. GOTARDO Municipal Local Government Operations Officer Municipality of Sierra Bullones, Bohol Dear Ms. Gotardo: s. 202 This has reference to your letter dated 28 January 2019 requesting for the opinion on propriety of charging the electrical system upgrading for the municipal building to Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Fund (LDRRMF) under "Preparedness DRRM Thematic Area." Per your letter, the Municipal government of Sierra Bullones, Bohol purchased a generator set for use of municipal hall. It was charged to LDRRMF fund under "Preparedness DRRM Thematic Area" category. Now, the Municipality of Sierra Bullones, Bohol asks whether or not it is allowed to charge the electrical system upgrading of the municipal building project to LDRRMF under the said "Preparedness" category. Our view leans toward the negative. We find National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council, Department of Budget and Management, and this Department's Joint Memorandum Circular (JMC) No. 2013-1, entitled "Allocation and Utilization of the Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Fund (LDRRMF)," relevant. In item no. 5.2 thereof, it defines "Disaster Preparedness." It States: "5.2 Disaster Preparedness — the knowledge and capacities developed by governments, professional response and recovery organizations, communities and individual to effectively anticipate, respond to, and recover from the impacts of likely, imminent or current hazard events or conditions. Preparedness action is carried out within the context of disaster risk reduction and management and aims to build the capacities needed to emciently manage all types of emergencies and achieve orderly transitions from response to sustained recovery. The JMC proceeds with list of projects and activities under the Disaster Preparedness thematic category, to wit: "5.2. I Conduct of trainings on disaster preparedness and response, search, rescue and retrieval operations;

5.2.2 Conduct of simulation exercises at vårious levels to test plans and skills; 5.2.3 Development OT information, education and communication (IEC) campaign and information sharing between LGUs/communities and the national government; 52.4 Development of standard operations manual for Disaster Operation Centers; 52.5 Development and implementation of standard operating procedures (SOPs) for deployment, evacuation and coordination with rapid assessment teams, etc.; 52.6 Development and institutionalization of early warning system (EWS), information sharing among LGUs/communities and the national government; 5.2.7 Conduct ofdisaster risk reduction and management researches; 5.2.8 Conduct of mufti-stakeholders dialogue; 5.2.9 Development and conduct of regular review ofcontingencyplans; 5.2.10 Development of information and database generation; 5.2. I I Stockpiling of basic emergency supplies; and 5.2.12 Other programs or projects of similar nature and considered necessary. We do not find a solid ground on which the upgrading of electrical system of the municipal building may stand on in the aforementioned. However, we find that the purchase of generator set may be categorized as provided for in item no. 5.5.2 of the JMC, which states: '5.5.2 Equipment purchased or received from donations and used solely for disaster response and rescue activities shall be classified as "Disaster Response and Rescue Equipment. " Ex. Inflatable boats, Megaphones including Sirens and Portable Generator Set. " (Emphasis supplied). Notwithstanding the aforecited provision, it is our understanding that such purchase of generator set may be categorized aptly as Disaster Response and Rescue Equipment if it is portable and is used solely or to be used solely for disaster response and rescue activities and not merely for electrical system upgrade of the municipality. The generator set purchased or the electrical system upgrading, as stated in the letter, is for the use of municipal hall/building. To our mind, this is not a Disaster Response matter but a matter of general management of the municipal building. The generator device is expected to secure the municipality from abrupt work interruption due to power blackouts, which is not classified as local disaster. Until it can be proven otherwise or that

blackouts, which is not classified as local disast". Until it can be proven otherwise or that the generator set purchased is primarily for disaster response, our view is that such shall not be charged to LDRRMF. In addition, the projects and activities to be charged against the LDRRMF shall be incorporated in the Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Plan (LDRRMP), and integrated in the approved Annual Investment Program of the local government unitl and the procurement of supplies and equipment chargeable against the LDRRMF shall be subject to Republic Act No. 9184, otherwise known as the Government Procurement Reform Act.2 Notwithstanding, this is without prejudice to the findings and position Of the Commission on Audit considering it is the government agency with •the mandate and technical expertise to provide definitive ruling on the matter. Very truly yours, By the authority of the Secretary: L C. SACENDONCILLO, CESO 111 Undersecretary cc: RD LEOCADIO T. TROVELA, CESO IV DILG Region VII LL1.S/42K I Item No. 6.2 ofNDRRMC-DBM-DILG JMC No. 2013-1 dated 25 March 2013 2 Item No. 6.7 of NDRRMC-DBM-DILG JMC No. 2013-1 dated 25 March 2013
